1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:03,207
MyCampusView Academy.

2
00:00:03,257 --> 00:00:06,117
Robotics 101, video 12.

3
00:00:06,167 --> 00:00:08,900
Uploading and testing.

4
00:00:10,050 --> 00:00:14,483
This is the big moment: your robot follows a real line.

5
00:00:14,533 --> 00:00:17,010
Let us make a good track first.

6
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:24,001
Use black electrical tape about two centimetres wide on white chart paper

7
00:00:24,051 --> 00:00:25,328
or a light floor.

8
00:00:25,378 --> 00:00:30,560
Start with gentle curves, and keep the track away from table edges.

9
00:00:31,950 --> 00:00:34,671
Upload the line-follower sketch.

10
00:00:34,721 --> 00:00:38,740
Unplug the USB cable and switch the battery on.

11
00:00:38,790 --> 00:00:43,070
Place the robot with the line between the sensors.

12
00:00:43,120 --> 00:00:46,360
After two seconds it starts by itself.

13
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:52,352
On sharp corners, a gentle turn may not be enough.

14
00:00:52,402 --> 00:00:55,142
The robot runs off the corner.

15
00:00:55,192 --> 00:00:56,910
Watch what happens.

16
00:00:59,750 --> 00:01:05,245
Now we set TURN_SPEED to minus 80, so the inner wheel spins backwards.

17
00:01:05,295 --> 00:01:09,760
The robot pivots around the corner and stays on the line.

18
00:01:14,250 --> 00:01:19,177
When tuning, change one value at a time and write it down.

19
00:01:19,227 --> 00:01:22,780
If the robot overshoots, lower BASE_SPEED.

20
00:01:22,830 --> 00:01:28,100
If it misses sharp corners, lower TURN_SPEED, even below zero.

21
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:30,871
Let us recap.

22
00:01:30,921 --> 00:01:33,208
Make a safe test track.

23
00:01:33,258 --> 00:01:38,084
Start slow, then tune BASE_SPEED and TURN_SPEED.

24
00:01:38,134 --> 00:01:40,827
Change one thing at a time.

25
00:01:40,877 --> 00:01:44,180
Next: Troubleshooting your robot.
